the text Amendment before you um it looks at making some modifications for detached uh buildings basically our accessory buildings uh detach buildings on a residential property so the first part of it is looking at the definition for detach building um just making a small change into that definition uh right now the definition is a building having no common wall connection with another building basically what we're doing with that is um changing it so that it says a building having no common wall or structural attachment with another building um this makes it much clearer with what a building being detached is um the second part is adding a new section into our accessory building regulations that basically would require any accessory building to have any part of that building be a certain distance from another building to be to count as its own building so

as an example if you have your house and then you're going to place a shed on your property that shed must be a certain distance from the house to be counted as an accessory build buing for setback purposes and also following our um accessory building regulations if that shed is adjacent to the house it's going to count as part of the principal building when it comes to setbacks so a home has a 20- foot rear setback if you place that shed at the back of the house and it is adjacent to the house or within a certain distance which I'll go over the distances that we kind of have laid out um both in the staff report and

what Planning Commission had recommended um but basically if that shed is within that distance to the house say Planning Commission recommended that these buildings have to be 5T apart so if that shed is closer than 5 feet to the house it's going to require it to be 20 feet also from your rear line it'll have to match the principal building setbacks otherwise detach structures do have a different setback to the rear property line if they are completely detached in a certain distance which would be on in some cases on certain Lots it would would be 5T from your rear property wi so when we took this to Planning Commission staff was originally proposing uh the separation distance to be one foot so

any building that is one foot from another building would then be counted as detached um Planning Commission when they uh when we went through this process and had the public hearing they did recommend and change it to 5T um staff does believe that the 5 foot distance could cause a lot more issues on a property uh things can get a lot tighter for homeowners it might be harder to place an accessory building at least with the one foot um staff would have an easier time of having that enforcement of what is actually detached um so going into why we're even looking at this my first example um this was basically built it was supposed to be a detached garage on the plot plan it's adjacent to the house they were saying it was going to be separated by just a couple inches but we didn't have anything in code that said a couple inches wasn't detached wasn't sharing a common wall connection it was proposed to be detached when it was built well it's attached um

so we have these issues where now the setbacks are completely off um it's not meeting setbacks but it did get a building permit that permit was signed off by a planner um back when this was approved um um because at the time the planner wasn't aware that it was supposed to be fully detached and they added some additional documents after its original proposal or approval and then the next documents added something additional that the planner didn't catch and now we have not a detached garage but an attached garage that's not meeting a 20 foot rear setback uh so with this amendment by having that one foot separation from any part of the building it will be clear that you have a one foot Gap um

and that is from EES that's from any part of the building so if you have EES coming off the house and off of that shed those EES have to be one foot apart from each other that'll be much easier to enforce and regulate to make sure that the buildings are actually detached and not attached so that we don't end up with situations where now could there be a fire hazard because this is too close to the property line um another example this is a very old shed so yes it was built back in the day but you can see that little Gap up towards the top it's a couple inches further up it is attached to the house um

so there's another example of kind of things that we look at and then the last one same thing what was proposed to be a detach structure it's basically between the house and the property line it's now clearly physically attached to the house um but it was proposed to be detached so these situations do come up uh from time to time um but as staff when we have to review these to have something clear in the code that we can clearly enforce is much more helpful so that's why we propos the one foot separation so with any of these if they were required to have that one foot separation then you would be able to clearly tell that it is clearly detached and

